      "text": "To determine the value of \\( n \\) that makes the seven-digit number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by 11, we can use the divisibility rule for 11. According to this rule, a number is divisible by 11 if the difference between the sum of the digits in the odd positions and the sum of the digits in the even positions is a multiple of 11 (including zero).\n\nLet's identify the digits in the odd and even positions of the number \\( 854n526 \\):\n\n- The digits in the odd positions are: \\( 8, 4, 5, 6 \\)\n- The digits in the even positions are: \\( 5, n, 2 \\)\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the digits in the odd positions:\n\\[\n8 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 23\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the sum of the digits in the even positions, including the digit \\( n \\):\n\\[\n5 + n + 2 = 7 + n\n\\]\n\nAccording to the divisibility rule, the difference between these two sums must be a multiple of 11:\n\\[\n23 - (7 + n) = 23 - 7 - n = 16 - n\n\\]\n\nWe need \\( 16 - n \\) to be a multiple of 11. Let's find the possible values of \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\n16 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\n\\[\n16 - n = 11k \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\n\nWe test values of \\( k \\) to find \\( n \\):\n\n1. If \\( k = 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   16 - n = 11 \\implies n = 16 - 11 = 5\n   \\]\n\n2. If \\( k = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   16 - n = 22 \\implies n = 16 - 22 = -6 \\quad (\\text{not a valid digit, as } n \\text{ must be between 0 and 9})\n   \\]\n\nThus, the only valid solution is \\( n = 5 \\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( n \\) that makes the number \\( 854n526 \\) divisible by 11 is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
